/**
 * @module imagine
 *
 * Reads, writes, draws on and transforms raster images.
 *
 * `imagine` covers everything a program usually does with an image:
 * decoding it from a file or an upload, resizing and cropping it,
 * adjusting its colors, drawing shapes and text on it, combining
 * several images, and writing the result in whatever format suits.
 *
 * ```zuri
 * import imagine { Image, Font, LANCZOS }
 *
 * Image.open('photo.jpg')
 *   .thumbnail(600, 600, LANCZOS)
 *   .sharpen(0.6)
 *   .save('thumb.webp', { quality: 82 })
 * ```
 *
 * ## How the API works
 *
 * Almost everything is a method of [[imagine.Image]], and almost
 * everything returns an image, so operations can be chained. There's
 * one rule to learn first:
 *
 * - **Operations that change the size of the image return a new
 *   image.** `resize()`, `crop()`, `rotate()`, `flip()` and `pad()`
 *   leave the original unchanged.
 * - **Everything else changes the image in place.** Drawing, filters
 *   and compositing modify the image you call them on, and return it
 *   for chaining.
 *
 * Call `clone()` if you want to keep a copy of an image before
 * filtering it.
 *
 * ## Colors
 *
 * Anywhere a color is expected, you can pass a [[imagine.Color]], a
 * hexadecimal string, a CSS color name, a packed `0xRRGGBBAA` number or
 * a list of channels. These are all the same red:
 *
 * ```zuri
 * image.fill(Color(255, 0, 0))
 * image.fill('#ff0000')
 * image.fill('red')
 * image.fill(0xFF0000FF)
 * image.fill([255, 0, 0])
 * ```
 *
 * Alpha goes from 0, transparent, to 255, opaque, as in CSS and PNG.
 * The color space conversions come from [[colors]], so hue is in
 * degrees, and saturation and lightness are percentages.
 *
 * ## Pixels
 *
 * An image is 8-bit RGBA with straight alpha, not premultiplied, stored
 * row by row with no padding, so pixel `(x, y)` starts at byte
 * `(y * width + x) * 4`. [[imagine.Canvas.pixels]] returns that buffer
 * itself, not a copy, and it's the fastest way to write an operation
 * that this module doesn't provide:
 *
 * ```zuri
 * var buffer = image.pixels()
 * var total = buffer.length()
 *
 * iter var at = 0; at < total; at += 4 {
 *   buffer[at] = 255 - buffer[at]
 * }
 * ```
 *
 * ## Formats
 *
 * These formats can be read: PNG, JPEG, GIF, BMP, TIFF, TGA, WebP, QOI,
 * ICO, PNM and WBMP. All of them can be written, and so can AVIF.
 *
 * AVIF can only be written, not read. TGA files have no signature, so a
 * TGA image can only be identified by its file extension or by naming
 * the format explicitly. [[imagine.capabilities]] reports what this
 * build can actually do, so a program can check instead of guessing.
 *
 * Animated GIF and animated WebP images are read through
 * [[imagine.Animation]], and animated GIF images can be written.
 *
 * ## Drawing
 *
 * Every filled shape is turned into a polygon and drawn by a single
 * anti-aliased scanline rasterizer, and every outline is turned into
 * the polygon around its stroke and drawn by the same rasterizer. To
 * draw a shape that this module doesn't have a method for, pass its
 * points to [[imagine.Canvas.fill_polygon]].
 *
 * ```zuri
 * import imagine { Image, Color }
 *
 * Image(300, 200, 'white')
 *   .fill_rounded_rect(20, 20, 260, 160, 16, '#4f46e5')
 *   .circle(150, 100, 50, 'white', { thickness: 4 })
 *   .save('card.png')
 * ```
 *
 * ## Text
 *
 * Text needs a font, either loaded from a file with
 * [[imagine.Font.load]] or found on the system with
 * [[imagine.Font.system]]. Glyphs are positioned by their advance
 * widths, with kerning. Complex scripts that need shaping aren't
 * supported, and the `Font` documentation explains exactly what that
 * rules out.
 *
 * ## Memory
 *
 * An image takes four bytes per pixel, so a 6000x4000 photograph takes
 * 96 MB in memory, however small its file was. For images whose size
 * you don't control, [[imagine.probe]] reads the dimensions from the
 * header without decoding the image.
 *
 * ```zuri
 * var header = imagine.probe(upload)
 *
 * if header == nil or header.width * header.height > 40000000 {
 *   raise Error('image is missing or too large')
 * }
 * ```
 *
 * @copyright 2021, Richard Ore and Zuri contributors
 */

import @.animation { Animation }
import @.canvas { Canvas }
import @.color { * }
import @.constants { * }
import @.errors { * }
import @.filters
import @.font { Font }
import @.formats
import @.image { Image }
import @.strokefont { StrokeFont }

/**
 * Opens an image file.
 *
 * A shorthand for [[imagine.Image.open]].
 *
 * @param string|file source
 * @param ?dict options
 * @returns Image
 */
def open(source, options) {
  return Image.open(source, options)
}

/**
 * Decodes image data held in memory.
 *
 * A shorthand for [[imagine.Image.decode]].
 *
 * @param bytes data
 * @param ?dict options
 * @returns Image
 */
def decode(data, options) {
  return Image.decode(data, options)
}

/**
 * Creates a new image.
 *
 * A shorthand for the [[imagine.Image]] constructor.
 *
 * @param number width
 * @param number height
 * @param ?Color|string|number fill
 * @returns Image
 */
def create(width, height, fill) {
  return Image(width, height, fill)
}

/**
 * Reads an image's format and dimensions from its header, without
 * decoding any pixels.
 *
 * Returns `{format, width, height}`, or `nil` when the data is not a
 * recognisable image. Reading a large JPEG's header costs microseconds
 * where decoding it costs tens of milliseconds, which makes this the
 * right way to size up an upload before committing to it.
 *
 * @param bytes data
 * @returns ?dict
 */
def probe(data) {
  return formats.probe(data)
}

/**
 * Identifies image data by its contents, returning a format name or
 * `nil`.
 *
 * @param bytes data
 * @returns ?string
 */
def detect(data) {
  return formats.detect(data)
}

/**
 * What this build can do, as a dictionary with `decode`, `encode` and
 * `animated`, each a list of format names.
 *
 * It's worth checking this instead of assuming. AVIF appears under
 * `encode` but not under `decode`, and the formats that are compiled in
 * can differ between builds.
 *
 * @returns dict
 */
def capabilities() {
  var decode = formats.readable()
  var encode = formats.writable()
  var animated = formats.animatable()

  return { decode, encode, animated }
}
